<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Hincks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hincks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Hincks was an Irish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> clergyman, Egyptologist and Assyriologist, known for the decipherment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, he became a Fellow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> in 1813 and from 1826 was rector of Killyleagh, County Down, until his death. Working independently of Henry Creswicke Rawlinson, he deduced the essentially syllabic nature of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> cuneiform, and by 1850 he had concluded that Akkadian cuneiform was syllabic and its signs polyphonic, each with several readings. In 1857 he, Rawlinson, Jules Oppert and William Henry Fox Talbot each translated a new inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a>, and the close agreement of their versions settled the decipherment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-hincks.jpg" alt="Edward Hincks" width="330" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Hincks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Hincks was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> on 19 August 1792, the eldest of the eight children of Anne Hincks, née Boult, and the Rev. Thomas Dix Hincks, a Unitarian minister, orientalist and naturalist who helped found the Belfast Natural History and Philosophical Society. His younger brothers included Francis Hincks and William Hincks. Taught at home by his father and at Midleton College, he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>, became a Scholar in 1810 and in 1812 won the Gold Medal and Bishop Law's Prize for Mathematics. He was elected a Fellow over Thomas Romney Robinson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1819 Hincks was presented to the valuable but isolated rectory of Ardtrea in County Tyrone, which he resigned for Killyleagh. His light duties left him time for ancient languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> came first: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-champollion">Jean-François Champollion</a>'s decipherment he made discoveries of his own, and in 1835 he supervised the unrolling of the mummy of Takabuti at the Belfast Natural History Society, reading the hieroglyphs that showed she had been mistress of a great house. In 1839 and 1846 he published on Egyptian chronology, identifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> with the Menophres of the Era of Menophres.</p><p>His interest in Assyriology was drawn by Paul Émile Botta's excavations of 1842 and the finds that followed, among them the Library of Assurbanipal; he corresponded at length with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a>. Hincks argued correctly that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> had been invented by one of the earliest peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, later identified by Jules Oppert as the Sumerians, and passed on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>. The Royal Irish Academy gave him its Cunningham Medal in 1848. He also identified many determinatives and their readings, though some philologists suspected the decipherers of inventing readings to suit their translations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The 1857 test that answered those doubts was proposed by Talbot. Edwin Norris, secretary of the Royal Asiatic Society, gave each scholar a copy of the inscription, and a jury of experts compared the results. Talbot had made mistakes and Oppert's English left some passages doubtful, but Hincks's and Rawlinson's versions were virtually identical. Hincks spent his remaining years on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> and died at his rectory in Killyleagh on 3 December 1866, aged 74, survived by his wife and four daughters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Hincks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Edward Hincks</b>, Rawlinson, Jules Oppert and William Henry Fox Talbot worked out its principles in the 1850s, and in 1857 their independent translations of the same inscription confirmed their success.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Irish scholar <b>Edward Hincks</b> suggested in 1850 that cuneiform had been invented by speakers of a language other than Akkadian, and in 1869 Jules Oppert named it Sumerian.</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cork, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in Killyleagh, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.9,-8.47&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Cork on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4359 370 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Hincks"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4809 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4809 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.9,-8.47&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
